<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Canton of Uri</h1><p class="st-meta">1231 CE – 1798 CE · capital Altdorf</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Alpine valley community guarding the Gotthard road, a founding member of the Swiss Confederacy and home of the William Tell legend.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 853 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reuss-river">Reuss</a> valley of Uri to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraumunster">Fraumünster</a> abbey of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Zürich</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthard-pass">Gotthard Pass</a> was opened to traffic in the early 13th century, the valley gained strategic value, and in 1231 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-germany">Henry VII</a> bought it back from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and granted it imperial immediacy. In 1291 Uri joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-schwyz">Schwyz</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-unterwalden">Unterwalden</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-charter-of-1291">Federal Charter</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Free peasants governed themselves through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a>, the assembly of all men, which elected a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landammann">Landammann</a>. Wealth came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, transport across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthard-pass">Gotthard</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> service. Uri expanded southwards over the pass, taking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leventina">Leventina</a> valley in the 15th century. The legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-tell">William Tell</a> is set in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altdorf-uri">Altdorf</a>. The canton stayed Catholic during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The French invasion of 1798 brought Uri into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>. It was restored as a canton in 1803 and remains a canton of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Switzerland</a>, crossed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthard-pass">Gotthard</a> road and railway tunnels.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/canton-of-uri.jpg" alt="Canton of Uri" width="320" height="139"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_Kanton_Uri.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1231 CE – 1798 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Altdorf</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Uri</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1514&amp;focus=canton-of-uri" aria-label="Canton of Uri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4883 531 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Canton of Uri lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5203 787L5186 782M5203 787L5188 772"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5186 782h0M5188 772h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5203 787h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5203 787h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Altdorf. <a href="/atlas?year=1514&amp;focus=canton-of-uri">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
